**Concise Code Execution on Multiple Variables in C++**

To run the same code inline on multiple named variables in C++, you can utilize a loop or a function. Here's an example using a loop:

```cpp
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    int var1 = 10;
    int var2 = 20;
    int var3 = 30;

    // Define a lambda function to print the variable
    auto printVariable = [](int var) { std::cout << var << std::endl; };

    // Run the function on each variable
    printVariable(var1);
    printVariable(var2);
    printVariable(var3);

    return 0;
}
```

Alternatively, you can use a range-based for loop to iterate over the variables:

```cpp
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    int var1 = 10;
    int var2 = 20;
    int var3 = 30;

    // Define a lambda function to print the variable
    auto printVariable = [](auto var) { std::cout << var << std::endl; };

    // Run the function on each variable using a range-based for loop
    for (auto var : {var1, var2, var3}) {
        printVariable(var);
    }

    return 0;
}
```

Or, if you have a large number of variables, you can define a function to execute on each variable:

```cpp
#include <iostream>

void executeOnVariables(int var1, int var2, int var3) {
    // Code to be executed on each variable
    std::cout << var1 << std::endl;
    std::cout << var2 << std::endl;
    std::cout << var3 << std::endl;
}

int main() {
    int var1 = 10;
    int var2 = 20;
    int var3 = 30;

    // Call the function on each variable
    executeOnVariables(var1, var2, var3);

    return 0;
}
```

Choose the approach that best fits your needs based on the number of variables and the complexity of the code to be executed.

